deep.reconstruction:
Context. In the third version of 'Analysis situs' (1986), Grothendieck sets up an axiomatic, combinatorial-and-set-theoretic theory of tame stratified spaces. The data are:
- a set 𝓜 of strata, ordered by ≤ (incidence/closure);
- figures F ∈ 𝓕, each with a 'déploiement' F̃ (its poset of strata) and an 'Ombre' Omb F (its underlying support set);
- a relation ≪ ('is carried by / contained in the support of', also used for refinements);
- a compatibility relation ≍, meaning the two figures are sub-figures of a common figure;
- a relation ∥, which by its use here and on the next page behaves like 'disjoint, without contact'.

The previous page states Axiom C (compatibility criterion for refinements of sub-figures). Take strata X ≍ Y with X' ≪ X and Y' ≪ Y. Put L = X ∩ Y, and let X'_L = {X'' ∈ X̃' | X'' ≪ L} and Y'_L be the induced refinements of L. Then X'_L ≍ Y'_L ⇒ X' ≍ Y'; the converse is already known. That page also states the target Proposition for figures: if F ≍ G, F' ≪ F, G' ≪ G and L = F ∩ G, then F' ≍ G' ⇔ F'_L ≍ G'_L.

This page.
1. Set-theoretic picture. With H = F ∪ G, the supports form a hexagonal lattice:
   - Omb H at the top, over Omb F and Omb G, which meet in Omb L;
   - Omb F' and Omb L lie over Omb F'_L; Omb L and Omb G' lie over Omb G'_L.
   Each of the three squares is cartesian, i.e. an intersection: Omb L = Omb F ∩ Omb G, Omb F'_L = Omb F' ∩ Omb L, and likewise for G'.
2. The same diagram holds 'itou' for the déploiements (the posets of strata H̃, F̃, G̃, L̃, F̃', G̃', F̃'_L, G̃'_L), with arrows for the inclusions.
3. Reading of the Proposition. Compatibility of F' and G' means X' ≍ Y' for all strata X' ∈ F̃', Y' ∈ G̃'. The Proposition says it suffices to check this for X' ∈ F̃'_L, Y' ∈ G̃'_L, i.e. X' ≪ L and Y' ≪ L. Since X' ≪ F already, this amounts to X' ≪ G and Y' ≪ F.
4. Axiom D (criterion for the disposition of refinements of sub-figures). This is the analogue of Axiom C for ∥:
   - setting: X', Y' with X' ≪ X, Y' ≪ Y, X ≍ Y, L = X ∩ Y;
   - already known: X' ∥ Y' ⇒ X'_L ∥ Y'_L;
   - postulated: X'_L ∥ Y'_L ⇒ X' ∥ Y'.
   For supports this is trivial: Omb X' ∩ Omb Y' ⊂ Omb L, so two pieces are disjoint as soon as their traces on L are. Grothendieck imposes it combinatorially so that the figure-level statement F' ∥ G' ⇔ F'_L ∥ G'_L holds. That statement, and its corollary F ∥ G, F' ≪ F, G' ≪ G ⇒ F' ∥ G', appear on the next page.

Purpose: a local-to-global ('check on the overlap') principle for gluing refined sub-figures. It is part of building a combinatorial category of tame figures, in the spirit of the 'topologie modérée' of the Esquisse.
